            <ArticleTitle>Enhanced external counterpulsation improves peripheral resistance artery blood flow in patients with coronary artery disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>405-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1139/apnm-2013-0309</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Enhanced external counterpulsation (EECP) increases coronary artery perfusion and improves endothelium-dependent vasodilation in peripheral muscular conduit arteries. It is unknown whether vasodilatory capacity is improved in the peripheral resistance vasculature. Here we provide novel evidence from the first randomized, sham-controlled study that EECP increases peak limb blood flow and improves endothelium-dependent vasodilation in both calf and forearm resistance arteries in patients with coronary artery disease.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
